    "summary": "LinkedIn Uses AI to Deliver an Improved and More Relevant Feed for 1.3 Billion Members What LinkedIn Wanted to Accomplish LinkedIn’s feed reaches more than 1.3 billion members. By early 2025, the platform was driven by five separate data retrieval pipelines. Each retrieval pipeline had its own infra...",
    "developer_name": "Ray Rike",
    "content": "AI to ROI Case Study: LinkedIn's Global Feed Transformation AI to ROI - By Ray Rike and Peter Buchanan Subscribe Sign in AI to ROI Case Study: LinkedIn's Global Feed Transformation How one LLM replaced five legacy retrieval systems and what it took to get there Ray Rike and Peter Buchanan Mar 31, 2026 2 Share LinkedIn Uses AI to Deliver an Improved and More Relevant Feed for 1.3 Billion Members What LinkedIn Wanted to Accomplish LinkedIn’s feed reaches more than 1.3 billion members. By early 2025, the platform was driven by five separate data retrieval pipelines. Each retrieval pipeline had its own infrastructure and optimization logic, serving different slices of what users might want to see. Data sources included a chronological network index, geographically trending topics, interest-based filters, industry-specific content, and embedding-based systems that generate data. The goal was straightforward: Replace that fragmented stack with a single, unified system that could understand professional context more precisely and cost less to operate. Specifically, the redesign needed to concentrate on three things: How content is retrieved How it is ranked How the underlying compute is managed The Challenges Five independent feed pipelines created massive real-time delivery and maintenance challenges: Each source had its own engineering team, optimization logic, and data infrastructure. As the platform grew, maintenance costs soared, and the multiple systems became harder to improve holistically. The legacy system lacked a modern approach to semantics . Keyword-based and collaborative-filtering systems struggle with meaning. For example, a member interested in electrical engineering who engaged heavily with posts about small modular reactors would not reliably see content about grid optimization or renewable energy, because the keywords don’t overlap. The old system matched surface signals rather than professional intent. Ranking posts didn’t account for how the content is used . Traditional models evaluated each post independently, scoring it as if it existed in a vacuum. They missed the sequential patterns in how professionals consume content over time, as the trajectory of interests tells you something about where a person is headed, not just where they have been. It was hard for new members to get started because they had limited engagement history and were effectively invisible to recommendation engines that relied on behavioral data. Shallow correlations from sparse profile data produced shallow recommendations. “Starting from our entire system for retrieving content, we’ve moved over to using really large-scale LLMs to understand content much more richly on LinkedIn and be able to match it much more personally to members.” — Tim Jurka, VP of Engineering, LinkedIn The Solution LinkedIn replaced its five data retrieval pipelines with a single LLM-based dual-encoder system : A shared language model processes both member prompts and post prompts , g",
