rump administration, issuing an injunction that halts the Pentagon’s designation of the company as a national-security supply-chain risk. Judge Lin of the Northern District of California ruled that the government’s measures “appear designed to punish Anthropic” and trampled the company’s First Amendment protections, a finding that goes well beyond the narrow procedural question of contract terms and strikes at the legitimacy of the administration’s entire legal theory. What the Judge Ruled Judge Lin’s injunction orders the government to: Stop applying the president’s directive barring federal agencies from using Anthropic’s technology Cease implementing the supply-chain risk designation. Submit a compliance report by April 6. Lin, a Biden appointee, found that the government violated Anthropic’s First Amendment rights by designating the company as a security threat. In the March 24, 2026, hearing, she described the administration’s bar for the designation as “a pretty low bar” , triggered simply becau",
